{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1777570676,"nonce":"nWVnDJYLuK2xO7L+xV9/BTsAtZ0=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABA-Gx4Q","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/115","origin":"github"},"title":"Install nlbwmon + populate path_subnets to enable top-talkers diagnostic on BizzyBoat (follow-up to ros2_network_monitor#20)","message":"## Summary\n\nDeployment-side companion to\n[`rolker/ros2_network_monitor#20`](https://github.com/rolker/ros2_network_monitor/issues/20)\n(*Add nlbwmon-backed top-talkers diagnostic to teltonika_monitor*).\n\nOnce #20 lands, this issue covers what BizzyBoat needs to do to\nactually use it:\n\n1. Install `nlbwmon` on each Teltonika (boat + operator).\n2. Populate `path_subnets` in the appropriate config so flows are\n   labelled with which path (vpn / wifi / cellular) they took.\n3. Verify the new `traffic_top_talkers` diagnostic appears in the\n   salmon-side `/diagnostics` recorder bag on the next deployment.\n\nBlocked on #20 in `ros2_network_monitor` — code change must land first.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nTwo unanswered questions from the 2026-04-29 deployment that the\nexisting `/diagnostics` stream cannot answer:\n\n- udp_bridge over Starlink runs at ~81% of the 5 Mbps Starlink uplink\n  (sustained ~4 Mbps) — knowing which topics or which peer is using\n  which path matters for trimming.\n- ~50–70 Mbps of WiFi-bridge traffic is uncategorised. Not udp_bridge\n  (~5 Mbps), and we use Zenoh (unicast TCP) rather than DDS multicast,\n  so the simple \"multicast bleed\" hypothesis doesn't apply. RDP can\n  plausibly account for 30–50 Mbps; the rest needs measurement.\n\nSee the analysis in\n`docs/logs/2026/2026-04-29_dev_logs.md` (timeline entries\n2026-04-30T13:30 — bandwidth attribution; and 2026-04-30T13:45 —\nrelative-bearing analysis), and the deployment issue #104.\n\n## Tasks\n\n### Install `nlbwmon` on each Teltonika\n\n```\nssh root@router.bizzy\nopkg update\nopkg install nlbwmon\n\n# verify it's running and has data\nnlbw -c csv -g \"ip,proto,port\" -o \"-tx_bytes\" -n 5\n```\n\nSame on `router.op`. Document the install steps somewhere\ndeployment-facing (this issue body or a short doc under the\noperator-side guides). The per-host data persists across reboots,\nso the install is a one-time setup per router.\n\n### Populate `path_subnets` in the BizzyBoat config\n\nOnce #20 ships the config schema, populate it from BizzyBoat's\nknown routing layout. Initial values:\n\n```yaml\nteltonika_monitor:\n  ros__parameters:\n    nlbwmon_enabled: true\n    top_n: 10\n    nlbw_grouping: \"ip,proto,port\"\n    poll_interval_s: 30\n    path_subnets:\n      vpn:      [\"192.168.22.0/24\"]   # salmon.vpn.bizzy.p11.lan = 192.168.22.142\n      wifi:     [\"192.168.13.0/24\"]   # salmon.op.p11.lan       = 192.168.13.142\n      cellular: []                    # populate if/when cellular gets its own subnet\n```\n\nWhere this lands depends on the resolution of\n[`#114`](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/114)\n(*Apply ignored_interfaces + diag noise reductions to BizzyBoat configs*).\nThat issue is deciding between **(a)** folding everything into\n`bizzyboat.yaml` and **(b)** loading the per-monitor YAMLs from the\nlaunch file. **The `nlbwmon_enabled` + `path_subnets` block should\nland wherever #114 chooses as the single source of truth** — not in\nboth. Sequence-of-work: resolve #114 first, then this issue applies\nthe new config to whatever location #114 settled on.\n\nRepeat the equivalent on the operator-side launch\n(`network_monitor_operator_launch.py` already loads per-file YAMLs\ncorrectly, so it just needs the new keys added to its\n`teltonika_monitor_operator.yaml`).\n\n### Verify post-install\n\n- Capture a fresh salmon-side `/diagnostics` bag on the next deployment\n  that uses the updated configs.\n- Confirm `teltonika_monitor: Teltonika: router.bizzy: traffic_top_talkers`\n  (and the operator-side equivalent) is present and lists top-N flows\n  with proto/port/path labels.\n- Run a quick post-deployment top-talkers cross-check against\n  per-interface byte counters (already in the bag) — they should\n  corroborate to within a small margin.\n- Use the result to identify the ~50–70 Mbps unaccounted WiFi traffic\n  and whatever's saturating Starlink, then trim/move flows\n  accordingly.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The code change itself (lives in `rolker/ros2_network_monitor#20`).\n- IzzyBoat / future-platform install — once this is documented, it\n  generalises by changing the host config.\n- iptables/nftables mangle-rule integration for hard per-interface-\n  per-port attribution (a v2 path on the `ros2_network_monitor` side\n  if dest-CIDR mapping turns out to be insufficient).\n\n## References\n\n- Parent: [`rolker/ros2_network_monitor#20`](https://github.com/rolker/ros2_network_monitor/issues/20)\n- Sequence-blocker:\n  [`#114`](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/114)\n  (*BizzyBoat config ignored_interfaces + noise reductions*) — settles\n  where new `teltonika_monitor` config keys live.\n- Deployment that surfaced the need:\n  [`#104`](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/104)\n  + dev-log entries 2026-04-30T13:30 and 2026-04-30T13:45.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}